WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:08.520
YUFENG GUO: The PC revolution
started this computing world

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.260
that we live in today.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:14.640
The internet came and
connected the PCs together.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:21.860
More recently, mobile has made
computing everywhere we go.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:26.010
AI is well-poised to
revolutionize, yes,

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:31.560
revolutionize yet again, how
we compute, how we connect,

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:36.210
and where we do all these
things and make it ubiquitous.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.180
My name is Yufeng and I'm a
developer advocate at Google.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.750
Today, I want to
convince you guys

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:46.500
that combining the power
of mobile and artificial

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.300
intelligence and machine
learning will make your systems

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:56.160
and change the world that we
live in today for the better

00:00:56.160 --> 00:01:01.560
and make for great experiences.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:07.230
Since 2000, half of the
Fortune 500 have disappeared.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:11.179
Only a few short years ago,
start-ups and companies

00:01:11.179 --> 00:01:13.470
would only occasionally
mention artificial intelligence

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:18.300
as this extra add-on to
their business process

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:20.880
or to this idea they had.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.640
But now, virtually every
start-up is an AI start-up.

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.530
They just kind of tag
that on the end, right?

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.820
Here at Google,
we've incorporated

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:38.070
AI, machine learning, into
many of our mobile experiences.

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:41.580
And hopefully you recognize
some of these on the screen

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.961
and hopefully you've found
them to be enjoyable.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:51.760
For doing machine
learning on mobile,

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.160
we still train on a server.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.340
And classically, we
think of training machine

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.740
learning models as a very
compute intensive action,

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:02.380
right?

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:04.560
How can we do machine
learning on mobile?

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:08.620
Mobile devices
are lower-powered.

00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:14.680
They can't flex out to
many, many virtual machines.

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:18.430
So, what I want to discuss
is the model where we still

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:20.530
do the training on
the server, but we're

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:23.620
going to do the
prediction on our device.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.590
And you can get away
with still using

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:30.340
mobile to call out to
your server for prediction

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.370
and just like a web
app or a website.

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:36.010
But if you put your
actual prediction,

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:42.100
put your machine learning model
on the physical device itself,

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:46.890
it can lead to some
amazing user experiences.

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:50.580
A great example that I like to
call out is Google Translate.

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:53.670
It uses on-board
machine learning

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:56.880
to overlay the translated text.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.020
This couldn't be done
if it was transferring

00:03:00.020 --> 00:03:02.870
basically the video of
what is being held in front

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:05.720
of the screen over the network.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.460
Sometimes you might
be in another country

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:09.890
and you don't have
data connection.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:11.960
Furthermore, even if
you had data connection,

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:17.870
you would want that immersive
and responsive experience.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:21.650
Maybe you're on a ship or
on a plane where there's

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:23.300
no network connectivity.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.880
Certainly you don't want
to be streaming images

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:30.390
over your satellite
phone connection.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:34.110
But machine learning,
it's kind of hard, right?

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:38.200
And making mobile apps,
that's no easy feat either.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.450
Combine the two.

00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:42.610
Well, that's a real challenge.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:46.670
So how can we make this easier?

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:52.100
Today, I want to walk
through a particular paradigm

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:56.060
that you can use to do
custom image classification,

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:59.180
so recognizing whatever
it is that you want

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.790
your mobile app to
recognize using a model that

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:04.200
will live on the device.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.430
So let's take a look at where
we're going to build today.

00:04:07.430 --> 00:04:11.750
So over here I have my phone.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:15.500
I've got it casting
via Chromecast,

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.329
so if we switch
over to the demo.

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:20.930
And so what we have here
is a camera basically.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:22.886
And I've got some candy here.

00:04:22.886 --> 00:04:24.260
You might recognize
some of these

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:26.764
from earlier in the conference.

00:04:26.764 --> 00:04:28.430
Some of you even might
have enjoyed some

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:29.660
of these peanut butter cups.

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:31.580
These are the milk
chocolate ones.

00:04:31.580 --> 00:04:35.450
And over here we have the white
chocolate peanut butter cups.

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:39.230
And you can see it
switching over naturally.

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:42.271
And then over here, we have some
just traditional Reese's Peanut

00:04:42.271 --> 00:04:42.770
Butter Cups.

00:04:42.770 --> 00:04:45.020
And you can see that
I can move it around.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:47.749
And the confidence at the
top is remarkably high.

00:04:47.749 --> 00:04:49.790
And I've actually got a
smaller Reese's Cup here.

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:54.950
So we've got Reese's
small and a Reese's large.

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:56.990
So that will switch over.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:00.140
And then just for variety
we got a pack of Juicy Fruit

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:04.460
here just for the people
who are allergic to peanuts.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:09.620
And this of course, all
works in airplane mode.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:13.350
So oh right, of
course it's not going

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:15.240
to work in airplane
mode in Chromecast.

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:24.350
So switching back to the slides.

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:28.090
Let's talk about how we
can build such a system.

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:30.880
And do it with a way
that doesn't involve

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:35.260
tedious data collection and
labeling and then spending

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:38.980
weeks of training on
a big server with GPUs

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:41.020
and writing distributed code.

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:43.210
And then we haven't
even talked about how

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:46.570
we're going to package
that up for mobile, right?

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:49.750
As you can see, I used
snacks from the conference.

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:53.080
I didn't know what snacks
they were going to have.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:56.720
I made this this week.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:59.750
It took less than an hour.

00:05:59.750 --> 00:06:02.300
And sure, I did some
preparation so that I

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.070
would be ready when I got here.

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:07.010
But there's no big system
running in the background

00:06:07.010 --> 00:06:10.230
making this happen.

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:13.310
So what does it look like
to go from training to app?

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:15.320
The first step is gathering
your training data.

00:06:15.320 --> 00:06:20.660
And in this case, we're
going to do it with video.

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:23.460
And sometimes the video
doesn't like to cooperate.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:25.990
So I have it--

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:27.750
whoops, go back.

00:06:31.430 --> 00:06:33.140
I have it here.

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:37.230
So what I'm doing
is literally taking

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:41.910
a video of what it is I
want to use as training.

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:45.210
Because what is a video after
all but tons and tons of images

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.200
being captured really quickly?

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:54.480
So if we take this video and
split it out into its frames,

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.806
we end up with lots
of training data.

00:06:57.806 --> 00:06:59.430
And since they're
all batched together,

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:03.360
one video is one folder full
of images to train with.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:05.460
So I just use FFmpeg.

00:07:05.460 --> 00:07:06.900
Literally a command line tool.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:08.940
Use copy-paste that
command line in.

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:10.620
Replace the file name.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:11.820
And put it in the folder.

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:12.720
Name the folder.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:16.950
In this case, Justin's Milk
Chocolate and make another one

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:18.520
Justin's White Chocolate.

00:07:18.520 --> 00:07:21.420
Make one for Juicy Fruit.

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:25.320
And what you get, you get
these pictures, right.

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:28.210
No surprises there.

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:30.572
Next step is to upload
them to the cloud.

00:07:30.572 --> 00:07:33.030
We're going to do our training
using cloud machine learning

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:38.130
engine and store our pictures
in Google Cloud Storage.

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:41.100
And that again is
a solved problem

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.980
that is basically just
running a few commands

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:46.740
to upload some files,
upload some data,

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:48.690
and kick off the training.

00:07:48.690 --> 00:07:53.710
And then after the training is
done, well, we'll get to that.

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:56.516
Let's talk more about
this training step.

00:07:56.516 --> 00:08:00.330
Don't let me be the one to gloss
over the hard work of machine

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:01.939
learning.

00:08:01.939 --> 00:08:03.480
So we're going to
go a little sidebar

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:07.460
because it's image recognition
and if you guys have background

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:10.320
in machine learning, you
know that we're going to use

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:12.610
convolutional neural networks.

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:15.330
So that's a lot of syllables.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:17.280
And I never won
the spelling bee,

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:22.380
but it took me a while to
understand what convolution is

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:25.600
and what machine learning was
doing to recognize images.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:29.070
So I want to use a little
analogy of a jigsaw puzzle

00:08:29.070 --> 00:08:32.250
I recently worked
really hard to solve.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:34.090
And this is an M.C.
Escher painting,

00:08:34.090 --> 00:08:36.330
but it's actually a box
cover of a jigsaw puzzle.

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:38.960
And usually when you
go to solve a puzzle--

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:40.919
I'm no jigsaw puzzle
expert, but maybe you guys

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:43.334
have some advanced techniques
you can share with me later.

00:08:43.334 --> 00:08:45.450
But I first separate
out the white pieces

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:47.220
and everything else.

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:50.310
This one was a real pain
because the edges were--

00:08:50.310 --> 00:08:51.540
there's a lot of white space.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.807
And those pieces take
forever to put together.

00:08:54.807 --> 00:08:55.890
So you separate those out.

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:58.350
And you say well, I know that
the white stuff is like that.

00:08:58.350 --> 00:09:00.720
And then the rest,
well, that's a jumble.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:02.820
That's what a jigsaw puzzle is.

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:04.890
But wait.

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:08.040
The roofs, the roofs
that are in this painting

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:11.230
are a very distinct pattern.

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:12.870
And so I can use
that to my advantage.

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:18.450
I can go and find the pieces
that look like this pattern

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:19.740
and boom.

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:20.910
I can piece that together.

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:25.482
Then I can say
well, these stairs.

00:09:25.482 --> 00:09:27.440
They've got some of these
parallel lines going.

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:30.620
And the railings have
these x's on the banisters.

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:32.330
I can use that to my
advantage and find

00:09:32.330 --> 00:09:36.590
pieces that are of that shape.

00:09:36.590 --> 00:09:39.390
And then put that together.

00:09:39.390 --> 00:09:42.206
So what does this have to
do with machine learning?

00:09:42.206 --> 00:09:47.270
A convolutional neural
network, in the abstract,

00:09:47.270 --> 00:09:51.740
can be thought of as looking
for these detailed patterns

00:09:51.740 --> 00:09:54.230
in the same way
that your eyes can.

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:55.670
It's looking at
these sub-patterns

00:09:55.670 --> 00:10:00.110
and then piecing them together
to come to conclusions

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:02.090
about the bigger picture.

00:10:02.090 --> 00:10:06.030
To piece these x's and the
lines of the stairs to say,

00:10:06.030 --> 00:10:07.850
well, there's a staircase there.

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:09.800
Or there's a roof there.

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:12.530
And piecing together
the roof and the stair

00:10:12.530 --> 00:10:15.710
and the hallway and
the clock tower,

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:18.710
you get the conclusion that you
have an M.C. Escher painting.

00:10:18.710 --> 00:10:20.751
Now I said, there were a
lot of white pieces here

00:10:20.751 --> 00:10:24.480
and so it took me a really long
time to even get to this stage.

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:27.320
So this is as far as I got
because the other white pieces

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:28.740
were just not cooperating.

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:31.670
So moving on from that
analogy, let's talk

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:34.100
about the actual thing
I used to train it.

00:10:34.100 --> 00:10:37.950
And it's using what's called
the Inception V3 model.

00:10:37.950 --> 00:10:40.580
This is a convolutional
neural network

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:43.490
that Google used
to win the ImageNet

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:44.930
competition a few years back.

00:10:44.930 --> 00:10:49.550
That an international machine
learning image classification

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:50.420
competition.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:54.580
And it is 48 layers deep.

00:10:54.580 --> 00:10:58.100
To give a little context
about what 48 layers is

00:10:58.100 --> 00:11:02.870
and how significantly large
that is, as recently as 2011,

00:11:02.870 --> 00:11:06.710
it was impractical to
train a neural network that

00:11:06.710 --> 00:11:10.470
was more than four layers deep.

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:12.630
So this was a huge leap.

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:15.750
The biggest network the
year before this network

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:19.500
won ImageNet was 22 layers.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:24.450
So it more than doubled the size
and ran through the whole thing

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:27.619
and can classify 1,000
different images.

00:11:27.619 --> 00:11:29.160
And so we're going
to repurpose that.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:31.740
We're going to take all
of the advancements that

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:34.800
were used to make this
possible to classify our candy.

00:11:37.590 --> 00:11:42.420
So what we can do is we take
that last layer of the network

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:45.900
and we're just going
to train that part.

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:50.350
That last step is what
separates out into buckets,

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:54.150
if you will, the
different classes that

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:56.170
are being recognized.

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:58.090
So in this case,
our five classes,

00:11:58.090 --> 00:12:00.300
the two different Reese's
Cups, the milk chocolate,

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:02.280
the white chocolate
peanut butter cups,

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:04.920
and our Juicy Fruit.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:06.960
So we feed in our
input data at the top.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:08.910
You see the Reese's
Cup at the top.

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:14.880
And we make the values in that
neural network all the same.

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:16.374
And not all the
same, all frozen.

00:12:16.374 --> 00:12:17.290
We don't let it train.

00:12:17.290 --> 00:12:18.330
We don't let it move.

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:21.720
We only let that last
layer be trained.

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:24.180
So those values get
updated during the training

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:26.800
and everything else
stays the same.

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:29.010
And this step of it,
this core piece of it

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:33.870
is a pretty well understood step
now, this retraining aspect.

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:36.041
And in fact, we have a Codelab.

00:12:36.041 --> 00:12:37.540
I would send you
downstairs to do it

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:39.360
but this is the
last session, right?

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:40.800
But it is online.

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:44.190
So you can do this for
your own problems as well.

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:46.080
And now, moving on
from that, I want

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:50.040
to focus on what we do to
go from a trained model

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:52.050
to a trained model on Android.

00:12:52.050 --> 00:12:55.890
Because if you come at this
from the naive approach

00:12:55.890 --> 00:12:58.410
and say OK, got the model,
let me put it on Android,

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:00.330
it will not work.

00:13:00.330 --> 00:13:01.470
At all.

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:06.030
Because the libraries that
are used on the Android

00:13:06.030 --> 00:13:09.960
version of TensorFlow are
missing certain operations.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:12.360
Certain operations
because you don't want

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:14.850
to do training on mobile,
as we talked about before.

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:16.680
Not much compute power.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:19.560
So we need to strip
down the model.

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:22.320
And that is nice because
the output of this training

00:13:22.320 --> 00:13:26.550
is 84 megabytes, which
is a real whopper.

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:29.580
So we want to size
that down a little.

00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:31.630
And we're going to
optimize it for mobile.

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:33.690
So let's talk about
this downsizing.

00:13:33.690 --> 00:13:36.210
There's a command
line tool called

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:37.590
the graph transform tool.

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:41.310
And what it does is,
it quantizes the model.

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:43.170
What is quantization?

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:46.530
What it means is that
there's the model, which

00:13:46.530 --> 00:13:49.680
is just a file filled
with floating point

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:51.300
numbers, decimals.

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:53.370
And these floating point
numbers are typically

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:59.190
32-bit so reasonably high
precision and reasonably

00:13:59.190 --> 00:14:00.941
space taking.

00:14:00.941 --> 00:14:02.190
So we're going to downsize it.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:03.930
We're going to
downsize it to 8 bits.

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:07.620
We're going to make
all the values 8 bits.

00:14:07.620 --> 00:14:10.560
But wouldn't that
affect the accuracy?

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:12.330
Fair question.

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:15.480
Thankfully, neural
networks are designed

00:14:15.480 --> 00:14:20.250
to be tolerant of fuzzy inputs.

00:14:20.250 --> 00:14:24.970
It's designed for a little
leniency and still be accurate.

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:28.410
So you can get all
4x savings in size,

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:32.590
with only a marginal
falling off in the air.

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:36.450
Which is great, because now
we have a 20 megabyte model

00:14:36.450 --> 00:14:39.360
that we can add to our app.

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:42.210
One additional consideration.

00:14:42.210 --> 00:14:47.440
How we add the model to
our app is up to you.

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:50.100
You can put the model
in the app itself,

00:14:50.100 --> 00:14:54.720
so that when your users download
the app from the Play store,

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:57.580
they will have the
model built in.

00:14:57.580 --> 00:15:02.040
And that means your app will
be say 20 megabytes bigger.

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:07.740
So you can also put the model
alongside your app as a file.

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:12.540
Then when they download the app,
they won't have that exercise,

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:16.020
but you have other
considerations.

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:17.310
Right?

00:15:17.310 --> 00:15:19.290
You need to then have
logic in your app

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:22.770
to download that model on
your own at some point.

00:15:22.770 --> 00:15:25.770
Maybe you only do it over Wi-Fi.

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:27.840
What about other advantages
and disadvantages?

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:31.174
Perhaps you say well, I really
want to keep my model secure.

00:15:31.174 --> 00:15:33.840
I'm afraid that people are going
to look at these floating point

00:15:33.840 --> 00:15:36.430
numbers in this file and
divine some kind of conclusion

00:15:36.430 --> 00:15:36.930
about it.

00:15:36.930 --> 00:15:39.690
So I want to keep it secure
and packaged in my app.

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:42.090
So you might go with
the first option.

00:15:42.090 --> 00:15:43.890
Or maybe you say, I
want the flexibility

00:15:43.890 --> 00:15:46.920
of being able to update
my model whenever I want.

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:51.810
Maybe just push down the diffs
and not update the app itself.

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:52.729
So then you say, OK.

00:15:52.729 --> 00:15:54.270
I'll put the model
as a separate file

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:57.760
and then I can have logic in
my app to do these updates.

00:15:57.760 --> 00:15:59.820
So this is a set of
trade offs that you

00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:02.100
have to weigh for yourself.

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:05.100
In my case, I've
packaged it with the app,

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:07.980
partially because it was more
straightforward to just deploy

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:10.250
the whole thing to my phone.

00:16:10.250 --> 00:16:14.090
And so yeah, and so
that's what we saw today.

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:15.170
We packaged it up.

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:16.440
We put it into the app.

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:18.800
And that's kind of our
full training pipeline.

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:22.240
From training to app or
from our case, video to app.

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:24.380
Turning that video
into pictures,

00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:26.210
training it in the
cloud, and then

00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:29.570
pulling down that trained model
and optimizing it for mobile.

00:16:29.570 --> 00:16:32.950
And then loading it
into an Android app.

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:36.500
And so we're pretty
much ready to ship it.

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:40.610
So at this point, I want to
step back, zoom out a little bit

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:45.110
and talk about what
makes all this possible.

00:16:45.110 --> 00:16:50.260
Because it's only
been in recent year,

00:16:50.260 --> 00:16:53.890
let's say, that
TensorFlow a, has existed.

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:57.340
It's only been around
for, say, 15ish months.

00:16:57.340 --> 00:17:00.310
And before that, how
would you have done

00:17:00.310 --> 00:17:02.650
machine learning on mobile?

00:17:02.650 --> 00:17:06.160
How long would that have taken
you collecting the training

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:06.910
data?

00:17:06.910 --> 00:17:08.243
Where would you have trained it?

00:17:08.243 --> 00:17:09.670
What would you use to train it?

00:17:09.670 --> 00:17:12.319
How would you deal with
the size of that APK,

00:17:12.319 --> 00:17:15.490
right, without having
abilities to prune down

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:16.501
the size of the model?

00:17:16.501 --> 00:17:18.250
And you'd have to build
your own framework

00:17:18.250 --> 00:17:19.875
for loading that
machine learning model

00:17:19.875 --> 00:17:21.509
into the device itself.

00:17:21.509 --> 00:17:23.050
So the reality is
what you'd probably

00:17:23.050 --> 00:17:25.060
end up doing is
hosting a prediction

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:28.030
service in the cloud,
on your servers,

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:31.840
and having that mobile device
call out to that endpoint

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:34.150
with, say a REST API.

00:17:34.150 --> 00:17:39.220
And doing that you incur
all the kind of distractions

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:42.010
that we kind of talked about
earlier, wherein you can't have

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:45.100
these immersive experiences,
where you can't point

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:47.380
a camera at something
and instantly have it

00:17:47.380 --> 00:17:50.870
show what it is.

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:54.080
So TensorFlow has really
changed the game there.

00:17:54.080 --> 00:17:58.340
And what's really great is that
not only does it work for what

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:00.170
I've shown you
today with Android,

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:04.910
but it is also equivalent
on iOS and Raspberry Pi's.

00:18:04.910 --> 00:18:07.310
So you can deploy
your mobile app

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:09.975
across all of your
favorite platforms.

00:18:13.090 --> 00:18:16.210
Another reason that
TensorFlow has been fantastic

00:18:16.210 --> 00:18:21.010
both for me and for all
of you is the community.

00:18:21.010 --> 00:18:25.480
So many people are making
examples with TensorFlow,

00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:28.780
creating really cool
use cases, answering

00:18:28.780 --> 00:18:31.090
each other's questions,
and supporting

00:18:31.090 --> 00:18:37.090
and helping each other grow
and build great experiences.

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:41.020
And that's only in the most
recent year and change.

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:44.740
And now that TensorFlow
is 1.0 as of mid-February,

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:47.800
you can use it with
confidence, in production,

00:18:47.800 --> 00:18:49.450
knowing that the
API will not change

00:18:49.450 --> 00:18:54.640
under you until TensorFlow 2.0,
which is so far down the road

00:18:54.640 --> 00:18:57.490
that I haven't even given
it a thought until just now.

00:19:00.010 --> 00:19:05.410
So machine learning on mobile
leads to awesome experiences.

00:19:05.410 --> 00:19:10.870
You can do it now
without having to utilize

00:19:10.870 --> 00:19:14.020
the pains of the past.

00:19:14.020 --> 00:19:17.380
And the tooling has gotten to a
point where you can essentially

00:19:17.380 --> 00:19:21.560
follow a recipe
and have it work.

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:23.140
And moreover, to
be able to do it

00:19:23.140 --> 00:19:27.010
for your specific application is
what makes it really powerful,

00:19:27.010 --> 00:19:31.450
because you could certainly
load up Inception, that network,

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:33.880
with its pre-trained
system that can recognize

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:36.130
1,000 different things,
but they're just

00:19:36.130 --> 00:19:37.120
kind of random things.

00:19:37.120 --> 00:19:39.820
Boats and oranges
and trees and houses

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:41.500
and streets and sidewalks.

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:43.960
But you care about
specific things, right?

00:19:43.960 --> 00:19:48.119
You want to build an app that
can recognize your things.

00:19:48.119 --> 00:19:49.660
So being able to
retrain it and being

00:19:49.660 --> 00:19:52.810
able to retrain it with
ease, with capturing video,

00:19:52.810 --> 00:19:55.900
without having to amass
terabytes of data,

00:19:55.900 --> 00:20:02.280
and spend weeks of time to
load, process, and train up

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:04.600
a model and then
still have to make

00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:07.090
that a consideration
about mobile,

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:09.430
makes this quite awesome.

00:20:09.430 --> 00:20:13.120
And so feel free to come
up at the end we can

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:15.610
play with the app that I made.

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:19.520
But that's really a toy example.

00:20:19.520 --> 00:20:21.910
You saw the way I was
capturing that video.

00:20:21.910 --> 00:20:24.287
I was just kind of walking
around, taking the video,

00:20:24.287 --> 00:20:25.870
trying to do it on
a white background,

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:27.578
because I knew the
podium would be white.

00:20:27.578 --> 00:20:30.080
But in your real case
example, real world example,

00:20:30.080 --> 00:20:33.610
you would perhaps do it with
a variety of backgrounds.

00:20:33.610 --> 00:20:36.880
Maybe in shade and in sunlight.

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:40.840
Maybe on white surfaces, on
carpet, on wood, on metal.

00:20:40.840 --> 00:20:43.360
Think about where your
users might see the things

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:45.989
that you want to recognize.

00:20:52.090 --> 00:20:58.420
So that's kind of the whole
process as a training pipeline.

00:20:58.420 --> 00:21:01.540
And it allows you to
think of it as something

00:21:01.540 --> 00:21:05.440
that you don't have to have
a huge burden on anymore.

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:08.500
You can basically pour
the data in at the top

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:11.290
and then have machine
learning in an app,

00:21:11.290 --> 00:21:13.480
coming out the other side.

00:21:13.480 --> 00:21:17.230
It's no longer a problem where
you say, well, let's see.

00:21:17.230 --> 00:21:18.890
If we're going to
make this work,

00:21:18.890 --> 00:21:23.350
we need money to hire a team of
PhD's to think about how we can

00:21:23.350 --> 00:21:26.050
recognize our specific product.

00:21:26.050 --> 00:21:26.890
They're very subtle.

00:21:26.890 --> 00:21:31.000
Some of them are only a little
bit different from the others.

00:21:31.000 --> 00:21:33.160
Inception can take care of that.

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:37.150
The model itself notices
the most minute differences.

00:21:37.150 --> 00:21:40.030
And hopefully many,
if not all of you,

00:21:40.030 --> 00:21:41.980
have had a chance to
wander a little bit

00:21:41.980 --> 00:21:45.100
about around the third
floor during the conference

00:21:45.100 --> 00:21:48.670
where we have the example
with the candy sorter, which

00:21:48.670 --> 00:21:53.140
is based on a Japanese
farmer who built basically

00:21:53.140 --> 00:21:58.780
this system but on a Raspberry
Pi to sort his cucumbers.

00:21:58.780 --> 00:22:01.320
And I don't know
about you, but I

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:03.390
can't tell the difference
between cucumbers.

00:22:03.390 --> 00:22:05.880
They all kind of look the same.

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:07.980
Even at the store, there's
like normal cucumbers

00:22:07.980 --> 00:22:09.090
and then they have
the fancy ones

00:22:09.090 --> 00:22:10.800
that are called like
European cucumbers.

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:12.180
And they're like
longer and thin.

00:22:12.180 --> 00:22:13.500
But they're all
packaged in plastic,

00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:15.083
so I never know
whether or not they're

00:22:15.083 --> 00:22:16.680
more fresh or less fresh.

00:22:16.680 --> 00:22:19.140
And in Japan, there's tons of
different types of cucumbers

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:21.150
and this guy is growing
them all together.

00:22:21.150 --> 00:22:24.630
And before, he had to
categorize these all himself

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:27.780
because you sell them to
different places, right?

00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:30.000
And now he has this system
that does it for him.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:33.210
And in that situation, you have
a very controlled environment.

00:22:33.210 --> 00:22:36.180
He knows the cucumbers will
come down this conveyor belt.

00:22:36.180 --> 00:22:40.500
He's got the camera and he will
see it in this orientation.

00:22:40.500 --> 00:22:44.490
In my situation, I knew that
I would be pointing my camera

00:22:44.490 --> 00:22:48.800
at the podium, at an angle
with a white background,

00:22:48.800 --> 00:22:50.460
under bright lights.

00:22:50.460 --> 00:22:54.180
So when I trained my model,
I found a white table

00:22:54.180 --> 00:22:57.600
with bright lights and
pointed my camera in my video

00:22:57.600 --> 00:22:58.680
at my objects.

00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:01.260
I walked around in a
circle to cover the angles,

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:06.040
but I didn't do much else
because I didn't need to.

00:23:06.040 --> 00:23:10.120
So you can solve kind of
a whole range of problems

00:23:10.120 --> 00:23:13.570
depending on your
particular use case.

00:23:13.570 --> 00:23:15.310
And so thinking
through this problem,

00:23:15.310 --> 00:23:20.140
in this model, in this mode,
wherein you don't necessarily

00:23:20.140 --> 00:23:22.600
have to completely
start from scratch,

00:23:22.600 --> 00:23:24.580
but just thinking
about a few key points

00:23:24.580 --> 00:23:28.222
can help with the
design considerations,

00:23:28.222 --> 00:23:30.430
so to speak, and save a lot
of trouble down the road.

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:35.200
And so I mentioned
that there's a way

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:37.970
to retrain this, or
do this online, right,

00:23:37.970 --> 00:23:40.960
because the conference
is at a close.

00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:43.722
And so I have a short
link for you there.

00:23:43.722 --> 00:23:44.680
You can do this online.

00:23:44.680 --> 00:23:46.420
That will guide you
through the process

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:49.030
of retraining the network so
then you just have something

00:23:49.030 --> 00:23:51.240
like a computer that does this.

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:53.350
And for the mobile
side of things,

00:23:53.350 --> 00:23:56.410
if you go over to
TensorFlow.org/mobile,

00:23:56.410 --> 00:23:59.410
it will point you to
the Android, the iOS,

00:23:59.410 --> 00:24:03.310
and the Raspberry Pi, GitHub
examples that you can literally

00:24:03.310 --> 00:24:10.100
clone, build, and deploy,
straight up, no code changes.

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:12.140
And then once you
have that working,

00:24:12.140 --> 00:24:14.120
you can connect
the two together.

00:24:14.120 --> 00:24:17.150
You can take the transfer
learning, that Inception,

00:24:17.150 --> 00:24:18.440
that first Codelab.

00:24:18.440 --> 00:24:20.420
Take the output of that.

00:24:20.420 --> 00:24:22.880
Run the graph transform tool.

00:24:22.880 --> 00:24:26.150
And then replace the graph
that was in your Android assets

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:28.800
folder with that one.

00:24:28.800 --> 00:24:31.370
And then finally you have the
Cloud Machine Learning resource

00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:34.760
there to learn more
about that and how

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:37.770
to get that part working,
though the Codelab up to the top

00:24:37.770 --> 00:24:40.650
does talk about that.

00:24:40.650 --> 00:24:44.190
So with that, I want
to thank you guys.

